# Technical Specification: ESP32-C3 Intercom Signal Generator (Calibrator)

Standalone bench device that simulates a real dual-polar (±4 V AC) intercom call signal for safely calibrating and debugging custom receivers/handsets.

**Firmware:** [`intercom-signal-generator.ino`](intercom-signal-generator.ino)

---

## 1. System Architecture

To simulate a classic analog coordinate intercom call line (e.g., Urmet 1132), the generator produces an amplitude-modulated signal:

| Parameter | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| Carrier | **800 Hz** square wave (fixed) |
| Rhythm | **250 ms note** + **120 ms pause** (~2.7 notes/s, audible gaps) |
| Power-up | Signal starts automatically (no button) |
| Target amplitude | ±4 V AC peak-to-peak (via AC coupling, see §1.2) |

### 1.1. Note / Pause Envelope (Not Mains Hum)

Fast 100 Hz gating sounds like **220 V mains buzz** — the ear cannot resolve separate pitches. For **distinct 800 Hz notes**, the gate must stay open long enough to hear the tone (~200+ ms), then a **100+ ms silence**:

```
Cycle (370 ms):
  0 – 250 ms:   800 Hz ON   (note)
  250 – 370 ms: OFF         (pause)
```

Gating runs on **`esp_timer`** (1 ms tick), independent of OLED refresh.

Tune `NOTE_US` / `PAUSE_US` in firmware if needed.

### 1.2. Single-Supply to Dual-Polar Conversion (AC Coupling)

An N-channel MOSFET (IRLB8743) acts as a low-side switch (0 V … +Vcc unipolar pulses). A series electrolytic capacitor C1 blocks DC offset and passes only the AC component, shifting the baseline to virtual zero → clean ±4 V waveform.

